/** \page build_page Building, Flashing & Provisioning

For memory allocation information, refer to \ref memmap_page .

\section generating_keypair Generating keypair for package signing and validation

This step needs to completed once, when setting up the development environment.

-# In a directory external to the git repo, e.g. in `/mnt/keys/` : `nrfutil keys generate private_key.pem`
-# Edit your bashrc file and add the following line to the end: `export PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=/mnt/keys/private_key.pem`
-# Reload shell config: `source ~/.bashrc`  
-# Verify key file `nrfutil keys display --key sk --format hex $PRIVATE_KEY_PATH`
-# Write out corresponding public key `nrfutil keys display --key pk --format code $(PRIVATE_KEY_PATH) --out_file /workspace/firmware/bootloader/src/public_key.c` . Note: you may need to delete the output file before running this command if it already exists.


\section steps_before_build Steps before Build

\subsection debug_enabled Set UART debug enabled (dev) or disabled (release)

<b>NB: always ensure that `DEBUG_DISABLE_UART` is enabled when building release firmware; failing to do so can lead to excessive power draw.</b>

In the app Makefile, modify

`# CFLAGS += -DDEBUG_DISABLE_UART # Uncomment to disable UART debug output for production`

This option removes UART debugging functionality for production, leaving only RTT logging enabled.

For dev purposes, this line can remain commented out to enable UART logging.

For release, uncomment this line.

\subsection set_version Set the hardware and firmware versions

-# Set the desired application version in `firmware/app/common/version.h`
-# Set the desired target hardware version as `HW_VERSION_STRING` in `/firmware/app/Makefile`

\section release_process Simple Build & Flash Procedure (Recommended method)

The below steps document the simple, script-based build and flash procedure. This is the RECOMMENDED PROCEDURE to accomplish building and flashing for both develop and release.

<b>NB: always ensure that a valid SEGGER programmer is connected to the device before attempting to flash or erase it</b>

Once you have completed this procedure, subsequent re-flashing of the app can be done using the `make flash` command in the `/firmware/app` directory.

\subsection script_release_process Procedure

Run the `build_flash.sh` script in the root of the repository, which will perform all of the build and flash steps automatically. This script supports the following flags:

- `-d` or `--dev` enables Develop Mode. If develop mode is enabled, readback protection will not be enabled
- `-t` or `--test` enables Test Mode. In Test mode, the hard-coded default BLE passkey will be used instead of generating a random passkey

For example, during development, use `build_flash.sh -d -t`, and during release, simply use `build_flash.sh`.

\section release_process_manual Manual Build & Flash Procedure (Alternative method)

The below steps document the manual build and flash procedure. This is an ALTERNATIVE PROCEDURE to accomplish the same as the simple, script-based process detailed above.

<b>NB: always ensure that a valid SEGGER programmer is connected to the device before attempting to flash or erase the it</b>

Once you have completed this procedure, subsequent re-flashing of the app can be done using the `make flash` command in the `/firmware/app` directory.

\subsection build_bootloader Build and flash the bootloader and softdevice

-# Erase flash. In `firmware/bootloader` run `make -j8 CONFIGURATION=release erase`.
-# Clean bootloader build. In `firmware/bootloader` run `make -j8 CONFIGURATION=release clean` (or set CONFIGURATION to `debug` if required)
-# Flash bootloader. In `firmware/bootloader` run `make -j8 CONFIGURATION=release flash` (or set CONFIGURATION to `debug` if required)
-# Flash softdevice. In `firmware/bootloader` run `make -j8 CONFIGURATION=release flash_softdevice` (or set CONFIGURATION to `debug` if required)

\subsection provision_key Provision the BLE passkey (PIN)

-# In `tools` run `./provision_pin` . If line endings were inadvertently changed to Windows-style, it may be necessary to run `dos2unix provision_pin` first. If you wish to use a static pin, edit the script such that the `PIN` variable is set to a constant rather than being generated randomly.
-# `nrfjrprog --reset`

\subsection build_app Flash the application + settings

-# In `firmware/app` run `make flash` .

<b>NB: always verify that DFU functionality still works correctly whenever firmware changes have been made</b>

\section dfu Preparing and uploading a DFU package

-# In `firmware/app` run `make package`
-# Copy the generated zip package from `/firmware/app/bin/` to a BLE-enabled Android or iOS phone
-# Connect the device to the charger.
-# Ensure that the device is bonded to the phone running the nRFConnect app
-# Scan for devices in the nRFConnect app
-# Select `CONNECT` on the `Validose DFU` device in the nRFConnect app (bonding may be required first)
-# Select the `DFU` icon in the upper-right corner of the nRFConnect app
-# Select `Distribution packet (ZIP)` and click OK
-# Select the correct ZIP file and wait for the DFU to complete
-# nRFConnect will show a banner indicating `UPLOADING... NOT BONDED` while the transfer is underway
-# On completion, nRFConnect will show a toast message indicating success. NOTE: The "UPLOADING" banner may not disappear after. This seems to be a bug in nRFConnect.
    
*/


